6.3 Lookup Query Schema
A lookup query has:

query_string. What the user typed.

query_type. One of name, identifier, description, partial.

library_filter. If provided, the lookup is restricted to these libraries.

field_filter. If provided, only these fields are returned.

confidence_threshold. The minimum confidence for a match. Default 0.5.

max_results. The maximum number of matches. Default 10.

6.4 Lookup Result Schema
A lookup result has:

query_string.

matches. An array of match objects.

Each match object has:

entry_id.

library_id.

confidence. A number between 0 and 1.

match_type. One of exact, fuzzy, alias.

entry_fields. The field values.

field_filter_applied. A boolean.

6.5 The Lookup Algorithm
The lookup algorithm has four steps.

Step 1. Normalise the query string.

Lowercase, trim, remove punctuation, expand known abbreviations.

Step 2. Search the alias index.

Each entry has an alias list, including its name, its identifiers, and common variants.

If the normalised query matches an alias exactly, return that entry with confidence 1.0.

Step 3. Search by string similarity.

For each entry, compute a string similarity score between the normalised query and the entry's name and identifiers.

Use a similarity algorithm such as Jaro-Winkler or Levenshtein.

Keep the entries above the confidence threshold.

Step 4. Rank and return.

Sort the matches by confidence.

Apply the library filter and the field filter.

Apply the maximum results limit.

Return the matches.

6.6 The Disambiguation Rules
When more than one entry matches, the disambiguator ranks them and returns all of them above the confidence threshold.

The calling app can then present them to the user, or choose automatically if one match is clearly better.

The ranking uses three factors.

String similarity. Higher similarity means higher confidence.

Alias match. An exact alias match means higher confidence.

Cross-library agreement. If the same name appears in multiple libraries and the entries agree, the confidence is higher.

6.7 The Fill-in Rules
The fill-in engine takes a matched entry and returns its field values.

The rules are:

Return only the fields requested in the field filter, if a filter is provided.

Return the fields in the order the calling app requests them.

Return the confidence score alongside the fields.

Return the entry ID so the calling app can record provenance.

6.8 The Submission Rules
The submission engine accepts a new entry.

The rules are:

Validate the entry against the library schema.

Require a depositor ID and a depositor affiliation.

Require a provenance record with a source and a tool version.

Set the moderation status to pending.

Record the deposit date.

Assign an entry ID.

Store the entry.

6.9 The Curation Rules
The curation workflow accepts an action from a moderator.

The actions are:

Approve. Set the moderation status to approved.

Reject. Set the moderation status to rejected.

Edit. Update the entry fields, keeping the provenance and adding a curation note.

Each action records the moderator ID and the curation notes.

6.10 The Library Version Rules
Each library has a version.

Each entry has a version.

A new library version can be published with a set of entries.

The rules are:

A library version is a semantic version.

An entry version is incremented when the entry is edited.

A library version is incremented when the set of entries changes in a way that affects the schema or the contents.

A new library version does not change the entry IDs. It changes the library version.

